        },
        {
          "unstructured": "Because of the ambiguity associated with accounting definitions of profit, the academic literature increasingly uses the term \u201crent\u201d to refer to \u201ceconomic profit.\u201d \u201cRent\u201d is the surplus of revenue over the \u201creal\u201d or \u201copportunity\u201d cost of the resources used in generating that revenue. The \u201creal\u201d or \u201copportunity\u201d cost of a resource is the revenue it can generate when put to an alternative use in the firm or the price which it can be sold for.",
          "key": "bibr11-41166664",
          "intents": [],
          "contexts": [],
          "source": "crossref"
        },
        {
          "year": 1982,
          "author": "Baumol W.J.",
          "key": "bibr12-41166664",
          "intents": [],
          "contexts": [],
          "source": "crossref"
        },
        {
          "unstructured": "In economist's jargon, such jointly owned resources are \u201cpublic goods\u201d\u2014their benefits can be extended to additional firms at negligible marginal cost.",
          "key": "bibr13-41166664",
          "intents": [],
          "contexts": [],
          "source": "crossref"
        },
